version\\n\\nUnknown — have not found a version where this works via a custom picker.\\n\\n### Does the issue also appear in Chromium / Google Chrome?\\n\\nI don't know how to test\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `session.setDisplayMediaRequestHandler` is used with `useSystemPicker: false` (i.e. the app supplies its own picker UI and resolves the callback with a `desktopCapturer` source plus `audio: 'loopback'` or `audio: 'loopbackWithMute'`), the resulting audio `MediaStreamTrack` should actually capture system audio, the same way it does when `useSystemPicker: true` is used instead.\\n\\n### Actual Behavior\\n\\nThe audio track is created (`kind: \\\"audio\\\"`, `label: \\\"System audio\\\"`), so the API call itself succeeds, but the track's `readyState` is `\\\"ended\\\"` from the very first observation, and no audio data is ever captured — an `AnalyserNode` attached to the track reports `peakAmplitude: 0` at every sample, forever. The video track from the same `getDisplayMedia()` call works fine (`readyState: \\\"live\\\"`, frames render normally) — only the audio side fails.\\n\\nSwapping the same request handler to `useSystemPicker: true` (letting Electron/macOS own the whole picker UI) makes system-audio capture work correctly and also correctly triggers the macOS \\\"would like to record system audio\\\" TCC permission prompt. With `useSystemPicker: false`, that TCC prompt never appears at all — which is consistent with the underlying native audio-capture session never actually starting, even though the JS-level API returns a track as if it had.\\n\\nThis makes `audio: 'loopback'`/`'loopbackWithMute'` unusable for any app that wants to keep its own custom screen-share picker UI while also offering a \\\"share system audio\\\" option — the only way to get working system audio is to hand the entire picker over to `useSystemPicker: true`, which replaces the app's own UI.\\n\\nReproduces identically on an ad-hoc `electron .` run and on a `Developer ID`-signed, hardened-runtime, notarization-skipped, `/Applications`-installed packaged build — so this isn't a code-signing or Gatekeeper/TCC-staleness issue.\\n\\n### Testcase Gist URL\\n\\nhttps://gist.github.com/anildigital/9a195654a687524fb544db62894466fe\\n\\n### Additional Information\\n\\nSteps to reproduce with the gist:\\n1. `npm install` in the gist's directory, then `npm start` (or `electron .`).\\n2. The renderer calls `getDisplayMedia({ video: true, audio: true })`.\\n3. Watch the on-screen log: the audio track appears with `readyState=ended` immediately, and the peak-amplitude readout stays at `0.0000` indefinitely, while the video track stays `live`.\\n4. For comparison, change `useSystemPicker: false` to `useSystemPicker: true` in `main.js` and repeat — the native macOS picker appears, the TCC \\\"record your system audio\\\" prompt fires, and after granting it, real audio is captured (non-zero peak amplitude).\\n\\n<details><summary>In-depth investigation</summary>\\n<p>\\n\\nWe believe the root cause is that macOS's system-audio capture (the ScreenCaptureKit-backed Core Audio Tap, gated behind the separate \\\"System Audio Recording Only\\\" TCC permission introduced in newer macOS versions) is only ever started by Electron when Electron's own native picker (`useSystemPicker: true`) drives the capture end-to-end. When an app supplies its own `desktopCapturer`-selected source through a custom picker instead, the `'loopback'`/`'loopbackWithMute'` audio request appears to be accepted and a track handle is handed back, but the corresponding native capture session is never actually wired up — no TCC prompt is ever shown for the app through this path, and `getUserMedia`/`getDisplayMedia` never delivers audio samples.\\n\\nWe were unable to find any workaround from the app side (no combination of Chromium command-line switches changed the result), and confirmed the failure is independent of code signing, notarization, and running from `/Applications` vs. an ad-hoc build directory.\\n\\n</p>\\n</details>\\n\",\n  \"form\": \"bug\",\n  \"labels_already_applied\": [\n   \"43-x-y\",\n   \"has-repro-gist\",\n   \"platform/macOS\"\n  ],\n  \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n  \"opened\": \"2026-08-09T16:47:59Z\"\n },\n \"form_fields\": {\n  \"electron_version\": \"43.3.0\",\n  \"os\": \"macOS\",\n  \"last_known_working\": \"Unknown — have not found a version where this works via a custom picker.\",\n  \"testcase_gist_url\": \"https://gist.github.com/anildigital/9a195654a687524fb544db62894466fe\"\n },\n \"repro\": {\n  \"gist\": true,\n  \"other_repo_link\": false,\n  \"code_blocks\": 0\n },\n \"bot_already\": {\n  \"asked_for_supported_version\": false,\n  \"need_repro_label\": false\n },\n \"issues_linked_by_reporter\": [],\n \"component_hints\": [\n  {\n   \"label\": \"component/desktopcapturer\",\n   \"matched\": [\n    \"desktopcapturer\",\n    \"getdisplaymedia\",\n    \"setdisplaymediarequesthandler\"\n   ],\n   \"in_title\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"label\": \"component/session\",\n   \"matched\": [\n    \"session.\"\n   ],\n   \"in_title\": false\n  }\n ],\n \"redirect_hints\": {\n  \"upstream\": [\n   \"chromium\"\n  ]\n },\n \"similar_candidates\": [\n  {\n   \"number\": 51571,\n   \"opened\": \"2026-05-09\",\n   \"score\": 112.3,\n   \"title\": \"setDisplayMediaRequestHandler useSystemPicker crashes after custom handler capture on macOS\",\n   \"shared_terms\": [\n    \"screencapturekit\",\n    \"usesystempicker\",\n    \"setdisplaymediarequesthandler\",\n    \"getdisplaymedia\",\n    \"picker\",\n    \"selected\",\n    \"capture\",\n    \"handler\"\n   ],\n   \"already_linked_by_reporter\": false,\n   \"excerpt\": \"### What operating system are you using? macOS ### Expected Behavior An app should be able to use a custom `setDisplayMediaRequestHandler(..., { useSystemPicker: false })` flow for one `getDisplayMedia()` request, stop all returned tracks, and then later switch to `setDisplayMediaRequestHandler(..., { useSystemPicker: true })` for a subsequent `getDisplayMedia()` request without hanging or crashin\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"number\": 52618,\n   \"opened\": \"2026-08-03\",\n   \"score\": 76.6,\n   \"title\": \"OSR useSharedTexture: details.texture is always null on Linux with the NVIDIA proprietary driver\",\n   \"shared_terms\": [\n    \"depth\",\n    \"delivers\",\n    \"hand\",\n    \"investigation\",\n    \"whole\",\n    \"capture\",\n    \"summary\",\n    \"separate\"\n   ],\n   \"already_linked_by_reporter\": false,\n   \"excerpt\": \"### Expected Behavior With `webPreferences.offscreen.useSharedTexture: true`, the `paint` event should provide `details.texture` as an `OffscreenSharedTexture`, with `textureInfo.handle.nativePixmap.planes[].fd` on Linux. ### Actual Behavior `details.texture` is always `null` on Linux with the NVIDIA proprietary driver. The `paint` event fires normally, but every frame silently falls back to the C\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"number\": 51972,\n   \"opened\": \"2026-06-12\",\n   \"score\": 75.8,\n   \"title\": \"BrowserWindow 'ready-to-show' never emitted when webContents.setZoomFactor() is called on a hidden window before first paint (regression in 40.x)\",\n   \"shared_terms\": [\n    \"plus\",\n    \"forever.\",\n    \"i.e.\",\n    \"stays\",\n    \"fires\",\n    \"introduced\",\n    \"workaround\",\n    \"reports\"\n   ],\n   \"already_linked_by_reporter\": false,\n   \"excerpt\": \"### Expected Behavior A `BrowserWindow` created with `show: false` emits `ready-to-show` after the renderer's first paint, even if `webContents.setZoomFactor()` is called on the still-hidden window shortly after `did-finish-load` — this worked on Electron ≤ 39. ### Actual Behavior When `webContents.setZoomFactor()` is called on a hidden (`show: false`) window in the gap between `did-finish-load` a\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"number\": 47980,\n   \"opened\": \"2025-08-06\",\n   \"score\": 75.6,\n   \"title\": \"`setDisplayMediaRequestHandler` must handle exceptions and user cancellation\",\n   \"shared_terms\": [\n    \"setdisplaymediarequesthandler\",\n    \"getdisplaymedia\",\n    \"wants\",\n    \"share\",\n    \"starting\",\n    \"triggers\",\n    \"changed\",\n    \"callback\"\n   ],\n   \"already_linked_by_reporter\": false,\n   \"excerpt\": \"### Expected Behavior ### Problem How can the app callback signal to the `getDisplayMedia()` caller that it wants to abort the window selection and deny the screen share? ### Reproduction 1. Some page JS calls `await navigator.getDisplayMedia()`. For the page, the call stalls here. 2. On the web, this would call the browser UI to select a window to share. In Electron, this calls the app handler th\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"number\": 47981,\n   \"opened\": \"2025-08-06\",\n   \"score\": 74.7,\n   \"title\": \"`setDisplayMediaRequestHandler` must handle exceptions and user cancellation\",\n   \"shared_terms\": [\n    \"setdisplaymediarequesthandler\",\n    \"getdisplaymedia\",\n    \"wants\",\n    \"share\",\n    \"starting\",\n    \"triggers\",\n    \"changed\",\n    \"callback\"\n   ],\n   \"already_linked_by_reporter\": false,\n   \"excerpt\": \"### Expected Behavior ### Problem How can the app callback signal to the `getDisplayMedia()` caller that it wants to abort the window selection and deny the screen share? ### Reproduction 1. Some page JS calls `await navigator.getDisplayMedia()`. For the page, the call stalls here. 2. On the web, this would call the browser UI to select a window to share. In Electron, this calls the app handler th\"\n  }\n ],\n \"similar_search\": \"BM25 over issue-corpus.jsonl, only issues opened before 2026-08-09T16:47:59Z\"\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012E1wCe3DiLJdzsJ31MAmB3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
